# Pinned jlink module list for the pine-lang desktop runtime image.
#
# Generated against JDK 21 (see ../JDK_VERSION) via:
#   jdeps --ignore-missing-deps --print-module-deps <exploded pine-standalone.jar,
#     with META-INF/versions/* module-info.class files removed first --
#     the uberjar carries stale multi-release module-info entries (bundled by
#     one of the merged deps, likely HikariCP) that make jdeps treat the jar
#     as a real JPMS module with unsatisfiable "requires", instead of the flat
#     classpath jar it actually is>
#
# --ignore-missing-deps suppressed a long list of genuinely-unused optional
# integrations bundled by dependencies (HikariCP's Dropwizard/Prometheus/
# Micrometer metrics backends and Hibernate integration, postgres's OSGi and
# Windows-SSPI/JNA auth support, jackson-dataformat's optional databind
# glue) -- none of these are in deps.edn, so their absence is correct, not a
# gap to fix.
#
# This file must ONLY be regenerated deliberately (JDK version bump, new
# dependency added to deps.edn) and re-verified against the M1 live-query
# gate -- never regenerated blindly on a routine release.
#
# jdeps-detected (static analysis of the actual bytecode):
java.base
java.desktop
java.instrument
java.management
java.naming
java.security.jgss
java.security.sasl
java.sql
jdk.unsupported
#
# java.instrument arrived with Jetty 12 (ring-jetty-adapter 1.15.5,
# 2026-10-08). jdeps attributes it to org.eclipse.jetty.ee.webapp, which
# pine-lang doesn't use, but it is kept because a missing module only fails
# at runtime in the packaged app. Re-verified on 2026-10-08 with Temurin
# 21.0.12: the uberjar ran on a jlink image of exactly this list, connected
# to Postgres 16, and served /build, /eval and the token check.
# Manually added -- jdeps' static analysis misses these because the paths
# are reflective/conditional, not direct bytecode references:
jdk.crypto.ec
# needed only if a user's Postgres requires SSL/TLS with an EC-based cipher
# suite; works fine without it against a plain-TCP local Postgres, which is
# exactly the kind of "worked in my first test" gap this file exists to
# catch before it catches a user.
java.logging
# defensive: Jetty/JDK internals occasionally touch java.util.logging on
# less-common paths (e.g. shutdown) even though this app's actual logging
# goes through slf4j's NOP no-op logger (confirmed via the M1 smoke test --
# "SLF4J: No SLF4J providers were found... Defaulting to no-operation").
